  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. Given that it is at rest when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$)  of the particle at time $t.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t).$",
